course: dictionary
lesson: 9838
title: Swimsuit and its many names
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A swimsuit is a piece of clothing you wear for swimming. Read the words around it to understand it: «Pack your swimsuit, a towel and goggles for the pool.» You wear a swimsuit in the pool and at the beach, so it must be clothing for the water.
explain: Different places use different words for the same thing. In Australia Jack says, «Stinkin' hot arvo, mate, off to the beach in me togs.» Togs means swimsuit, and arvo means afternoon. Other people say swimming costume, bathers or swimmers, and boys often wear trunks.
explain: Body safety lessons use the swimsuit to show which parts of your body are private. «Your private parts are the parts of your body covered by a swimsuit.» Nobody should touch them, ask you to touch theirs, or show you pictures of them. If anyone does, tell a trusted adult straight away.
fact: A swimsuit is clothing worn for swimming.
fact: A one-piece swimsuit is a single piece of clothing, often worn by girls and women.
fact: «Togs» means swimsuit in Australia, and «thongs» there means flip-flops.
fact: The «underwear rule» says the parts covered by a swimsuit are private, and nobody should touch them.
fact: Swimming costume, bathers, swimmers and togs are other words for swimsuit; trunks are swimming shorts for boys.
exercise: «Pack your swimsuit, a towel and goggles for the pool.» What is a swimsuit? | A kind of pool, deep and blue | Goggles to protect your eyes | A towel for drying your hair | *Clothing worn for swimming
exercise: Jack says «off to the beach in me togs». In Australia, what does togs mean? | A sandy beach | The afternoon | A pair of flip-flops | *A swimsuit
exercise: Which word for swimsuit is used in Ireland and New Zealand? | thongs | servo | arvo | *togs
test: In a body safety lesson, what does the «underwear rule» teach? | You must wear a swimsuit to school | Swimsuits must always be bright yellow | *The parts covered by a swimsuit are private, and nobody should touch them | Swimming costumes are only for the pool
test: «Un maillot de bain» is French for what? | *A swimsuit or swimming trunks | A pair of goggles | A yellow jersey | An afternoon
exam: Jack's «togs» and a friend's «bathers» both mean swimsuit, so why do people use different words? | Because a swimsuit is never clothing | Because togs means flip-flops in every country | *Different places use different names for the same thing | Because bathers is a longer word
